WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.060
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers. Episode 118. It's July 1st, 2026. The universe has been busy. Let's get into it.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:17.520
NASA is weighing whether to send a spare Mars rover to the moon while awarding nearly $600 million in new lunar lander contracts.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:24.840
NASA has awarded contracts worth nearly $600 million for four lunar lander missions to three companies.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:35.580
Astrobotic receives $297.9 million for two missions. Firefly Space receives $144.2 million for one mission.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.880
Intuitive Machines receives $148.3 million for one mission. The payloads will carry science instruments

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.920
and technology demonstrations to characterize the lunar environment. The selections directly support

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:57.780
planning for a future crewed lunar base. Next steps include final mission design reviews before hardware integration begins.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:06.000
While these new landers are being built, NASA is also considering repurposing existing hardware for an earlier lunar surface mission.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:14.280
The agency is evaluating the promise rover, a full-scale engineering model of perseverance for a lunar south pole mission.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.960
Promise would reuse hardware and software lessons from both curiosity and perseverance.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:31.280
The concept aims to accelerate surface exploration ahead of crude landings. Engineers are assessing how the rover's nuclear power system would perform in the lunar cold traps.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.700
Further analysis will determine whether the existing test unit can be repurposed without major redesign.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:47.160
Both stories show NASA accelerating lunar surface access. Meanwhile, the agency's human spaceflight efforts continue to face delays.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:55.300
A new NASA audit concludes the agency will need additional commercial crew flights to the International Space Station through 2030.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:06.520
The report highlights persistent technical issues with Boeing's Starliner spacecraft. It examines how certification setbacks have affected the overall crew rotation schedule.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:17.340
NASA continues to rely on SpaceX crew dragon vehicles in the meantime. The agency has not set a new target date for Starliner's first crewed flight.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:30.540
Follow-up reviews will track whether Boeing meets revised performance milestones. While astronauts keep flying on proven vehicles, researchers on the ground are learning how weightlessness itself affects the human body.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:37.640
NASA supported researchers built a ground-based model that reproduces the molecular effects of microgravity.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:45.480
The work shows that muscle cells lose strength through disrupted signaling pathways long before visible atrophy appears.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.420
Removing the constant mechanical load from gravity triggers these changes at the cellular level.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.860
The same model may help scientists understand muscle loss in other conditions on Earth.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:09.940
Data from the experiments are now being compared against astronaut samples. From the human body, we turn to the final stages of sun-like stars, where another kind of gradual change produces surprising results.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:24.100
Asymmetric mass loss during the final stages of stellar evolution can impart small recoil impulses. Over hundreds of thousands of years, these impulses accumulate and give the resulting white dwarf a noticeable velocity.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:31.140
The mechanism helps explain why many wide binary systems separate once one star becomes a white dwarf.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.620
The model predicts typical speeds reached after roughly 10,000 individual ejection events.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:52.740
Observers can now search for matching velocity signatures in nearby white dwarf populations. If you could watch a sun-like star in its final million years, you would see it shed mass in sudden uneven bursts rather than a smooth wind.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:04:01.100
Each burst carries away a fraction of the star's outer envelope in one direction, and the remaining core receives a tiny opposing shove.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:12.160
Over hundreds of thousands of years, these thousands of small recoil add up. After roughly 10,000 events, the white dwarf can reach speeds of several kilometers per second.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:21.920
The mechanism operates on time scales far longer than any single human lifetime, yet leaves an observable signature in the motions of white dwarfs today.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:33.460
We can measure the resulting velocities with precise spectroscopy, but no one yet knows exactly what fraction of the mass must leave asymmetrically to match the observed speeds.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:42.220
The same process may also explain why some apparently single white dwarfs show chemical traces of a former companion that was flung away.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:51.240
While white dwarfs quietly drift through the galaxy, astronomers are using a new space telescope to uncover hidden worlds much closer to home.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:59.880
A new mosaic of the Milky Way center from the Euclid telescope contains tens of millions of stars captured in 26 hours.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:14.280
Within the dense field, astronomers expect to detect planets through gravitational micro-lensing events. The telescope measures tiny temporary brightening caused when a foreground star and planet pass in front of a background star.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:28.600
Euclid was originally designed to study dark matter and dark energy, but is now also mapping galactic structure. The data will yield planet masses and orbital distances that are difficult to obtain by other methods.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.360
Additional fields will be processed in the coming months to expand the sample.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:40.580
Astronomers identified a new Earth-sized exoplanet through radial velocity measurements of its host star.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.320
The world orbits within the star's habitable zone, where liquid water could exist on the surface.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:58.720
Follow-up observations are planned to determine the planet's mass and density. The discovery adds to the growing catalog of nearby rocky worlds suitable for atmospheric study.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:07.360
The team used data from ground-based telescopes to confirm the signal. Further measurements will check for additional planets in the same system.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:18.300
The Keck Observatory has discovered a third galaxy lacking dark matter. The galaxy belongs to a linear structure possibly formed during a past galactic collision.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:28.400
Its visible mass alone appears sufficient to explain its observed motions. This marks only the third known example of a dark matter deficient galaxy.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:38.020
Additional spectroscopy will map the galaxy's stellar velocities in greater detail. Similar searches are underway in other filamentary structures.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:49.480
The Cavity Project mapped galaxy groups inside cosmic voids using a Friends of Friends algorithm. Results show the large majority of void galaxies have no companions.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:07:00.660
When groups do appear, they remain small and loosely bound regardless of local density. The findings challenge assumptions that environment alone drives galaxy clustering.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:07.580
Researchers are now examining whether internal processes such as gas availability play a larger role.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:13.460
Follow-up observations will target the few groups detected to measure their dark matter content.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:26.900
The European Space Agency has detailed instruments for a proposed and solidest lander. The mission concept targets the moon's south polar region, where water vapor plumes erupt from the subsurface ocean.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:38.740
Proposed instruments would sample plume material for organic compounds and biosignatures. The lander would operate in the extremely cold environment near the tiger stripe fractures.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:51.740
Mission planners are evaluating how to protect sensitive electronics from ice particles and radiation. A 2050 launch date remains under discussion within ESA's long-term science program.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:08:07.040
On January 26, 2026, the sub-millimeter array responded within minutes of a gamma array burst alert. The rapid response system recorded the event at millimeter and sub-millimeter frequencies for the first time.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:17.040
These early data reveal the initial evolution of the relativistic jet and surrounding material. The observations were published in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:26.500
The demonstration opens a new window for studying the most energetic cosmic explosions. Additional rapid response triggers are now being scheduled.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:39.040
Flight controllers detected an electrical current spike in one of the Canadarm 2 arms 7 joints on May 27. The anomaly prevented normal movement of the 17.6 meter structure.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:55.500
Crew members performed a series of diagnostic tests and mechanical adjustments during a spacewalk. The arm is now operating within expected parameters again. The repair extended the operational life of a critical piece of international space station infrastructure.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:06.460
Engineers continue to monitor the joint for any recurrence. Blue Origin is redesigning ground operations after the May 28 launch failure of New Glenn.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:19.520
Future vehicles will use a mix of horizontal and vertical integration steps. The change aims to improve access during final assembly and testing. An investigation into the previous flight remains active.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:29.440
Blue Origin has not announced a revised target date for the next launch attempt. New procedures will be validated during upcoming static fire tests.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:41.060
Venus reaches magnitude negative 4.1 and remains visible in the western sky after sunset. It sits roughly 15 degrees above the horizon an hour after the sun sets.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:51.180
Observers can locate it below the backwards question mark pattern of stars that forms LEO's head. No other bright planet currently shares that part of the sky.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:56.960
The configuration will persist through the coming weeks as Venus continues its eastward motion.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:09.100
Binoculars will show the planet's phase and nearby stars more clearly. Five participants from three space agencies finished the two week Caves Analog mission in an Italian cave system.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:16.260
The course simulates the isolation, limited communication, and technical challenges of long duration spaceflight.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:22.340
Crews conducted geological sampling, equipment maintenance, and daily planning under cave conditions.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:32.400
The training helps prepare astronauts for future missions to the moon and Mars. Data collected during the exercise will inform future habitat design studies.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:41.560
Additional cave sessions are scheduled later this year. New lunar hardware and fresh views of stellar endings keep the frontier moving forward.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:49.680
Keep an eye on the next round of Euclid data releases for more micro-lensing candidates. That's fascinating frontiers for today.

